<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/statistical-mechanics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Statistical mechanics is the bridge from atoms to thermodynamics: it explains pressure,
    temperature and entropy as <strong>counting arguments over astronomically many
    molecules</strong>. Its core result is Boltzmann's factor — the probability of finding a
    system in a state of energy E falls as e^(−E/kT). One exponential explains why air thins with
    altitude, why chemical reactions speed up with heat, and why your coffee cools.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Boltzmann factor — hotter systems can afford higher-energy states; the second curve is a colder world" widget="plotter" config={boltzCfg} />

  <p>
    At temperature T, the chance of a state costing E above the ground state is e^(−E/kT). At
    "one kT" of temperature the curve sags gently; cool the system (grey curve) and high states
    become vanishingly rare. Chemistry's activation energies, semiconductor doping and the
    population of atmospheric layers all read off this one curve.
  </p>

  <h2>Entropy, finally defined</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Boltzmann's formula:</strong> S = k·ln(W) — entropy is the logarithm of W, the number of microscopic arrangements that look the same macroscopically. Carved on his gravestone in Vienna.</li>
    <li><strong>Why disorder wins:</strong> there are astronomically more messy arrangements than tidy ones. The second law of thermodynamics is not a force — it is statistics with overwhelming odds. A shuffled deck never unshuffles for the same reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature redefined:</strong> 1/T is how much entropy you gain per unit of energy added. Things at the same temperature exchange heat without net entropy cost — that's the zeroth law, derived.</li>
    <li><strong>Maxwell–Boltzmann speeds:</strong> air molecules at 20 °C have a spread of speeds peaking near 500 m/s. Evaporation is just the tail of the distribution — the fastest few escape, cooling what remains.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The lesson of the field: <em>you don't need to track 10²³ molecules — you need to count
    properly.</em> Counting, it turns out, is most of physics.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Boltzmann (1877); Feynman Lectures Vol. I, ch. 40; Schroeder, An Introduction to Thermal Physics.</p>
</TopicPage>
